# Answer 5 · Should Women "Pull Each Other Up" into Leadership? (CSS 2020)
@py question 2020-5

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Explain the leadership gap and its barriers, weigh "pulling up" as useful corrective action against "preferential treatment", apply it to Pakistan, and argue that it is useful when done fairly and backed by institutional reform.
@ask Women need to be in LEADERSHIP to PULL MORE WOMEN into leadership roles
@ask Is "PULLING EACH OTHER UP" USEFUL, or INAPPROPRIATE PREFERENTIAL TREATMENT?
(1) Introduction: useful, if done fairly — it removes barriers rather than lowering standards
(2) The leadership gap: few women at the top worldwide and in Pakistan
(3) The barriers: glass ceiling, labyrinth, networks, stereotypes, care
(4) Useful 1: role models change who can lead
(5) Useful 2: mentoring, sponsorship and networks replace the "old boys' club"
(6) Useful 3: ending tokenism and building a pipeline
(7) Useful 4: women leaders change policies and cultures
(8) The objection: merit, resentment and stigma
(9) Further doubts: burden on women, solidarity assumed, queen bees
(10) Pakistan: Benazir, the caucus, women in bureaucracy and judiciary
(11) Drawing the line: corrective, not preferential — with institutional reform
(12) Conclusion
:::

## Decoding the question
The question sets up a **debate**: is the idea that women leaders should help other women rise **useful**, or is it **inappropriate preferential treatment**? You must explain **why** women are scarce in leadership, give **both sides**, and reach a reasoned **judgement**. Use the concepts **glass ceiling**, **tokenism**, **mentoring** and **sponsorship**, and Pakistani examples.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
"**Pulling each other up**" means women in leadership using their position to help other women rise — as role models, mentors, sponsors and makers of fairer rules.

#### ii) The argument
This is **useful**, not preferential, when it means **removing barriers** within fair, transparent procedures. It replaces the informal help men have always given one another. It becomes inappropriate only if women are appointed **because** they are women regardless of ability. It works best when backed by **institutional reform**, so that women's progress does not depend on a few individuals.

### 2. The leadership gap
Worldwide, women are a minority of heads of government, cabinet ministers, judges and chief executives. In Pakistan, **Benazir Bhutto** (==1988==) and **Maryam Nawaz** (==2024==) broke barriers, but in ==2025== there were **no women federal ministers**, and women remain rare in senior civil-service grades, the higher judiciary and boardrooms. Women are often a majority of medical and many university graduates, so the gap at the top is not a gap in **talent** but a gap in **opportunity** — what researchers call the **leaky pipeline**.

### 3. The barriers

#### i) Glass ceiling and labyrinth
Invisible barriers stop women at the top; **Eagly and Carli** (==2007==) described a **labyrinth** of obstacles throughout women's careers.

#### ii) Networks
Men benefit from informal "**old boys' networks**" that pass on information and opportunities — in clubs, families, alumni circles and party hierarchies. Nobody calls this preferential treatment, yet it has favoured men for generations.

#### iii) Stereotypes and the double bind
Leadership is seen as "masculine"; assertive women are disliked, gentle women dismissed.

#### iv) Care and culture
Long hours, travel and care duties penalise women; harassment pushes them out. In Pakistan, family expectations after marriage add a further barrier.

### 4. Useful 1: role models
Visible women leaders show that leadership is possible — what one cannot see, one finds hard to become. In India, after villages had women leaders under quotas, voters' bias fell and **girls' aspirations** for education and careers rose (Beaman and colleagues, ==2012==).

### 5. Useful 2: mentoring, sponsorship and networks

#### i) Mentoring
Advice and encouragement build confidence, skills and knowledge of how institutions really work.

#### ii) Sponsorship
Actively recommending women for posts matters even more for promotion.

#### iii) Networks
Women's caucuses and associations give women the connections men already have. This **levels** the field; it does not tilt it. Studies of corporate careers have found that women are often **over-mentored but under-sponsored** — given advice but not the backing of a senior person who puts their name forward.

::: fig mapping | How women leaders "pull each other up" — and what each step removes.
@left What women leaders do
@right What it removes
Act as visible role models => The belief that leadership is male
Mentor younger women => Lack of advice and confidence
Sponsor women for posts => Exclusion from informal networks
Build caucuses and networks => Isolation of lone "tokens"
Adopt fair, family-friendly rules => Structural barriers to staying
:::

### 6. Useful 3: ending tokenism and building a pipeline
**Rosabeth Moss Kanter** (==1977==) showed that women who are under about **15%** of a group are "**tokens**" — highly visible, isolated and pressured to conform. Bringing in more women reduces this pressure and creates a **pipeline** of qualified successors. Once women are a **critical mass** — about 30% — their presence is no longer remarkable, and they are judged as individuals rather than as representatives of their sex.

### 7. Useful 4: changing policies and cultures
Women leaders often introduce **anti-harassment rules**, **childcare** and **flexible work**, which help both women and men. Such changes keep talented women from leaving mid-career and benefit the whole institution. Pakistan's **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==), in which women of all parties supported one another, produced the harassment law (==2010==) and later laws on acid attacks, anti-women practices and honour killing.

#### An example
When **Benazir Bhutto** became prime minister in ==1988==, she appointed women to posts such as judges of the higher courts and set up **women's police stations** and the **First Women Bank** (==1989==), creating openings that women had not had before. These steps did not lower standards; they opened doors that had been closed, and many of the women who walked through them later rose on their own merit to senior positions. Opening a door is not the same as carrying someone through it.

### 8. The objection: merit, resentment and stigma
Critics argue that helping someone because she is a woman may pass over more able candidates, and that colleagues may assume women rose through **favour** rather than ability — a stigma that harms all women, including those who succeeded on merit. In a country where recruitment is often accused of nepotism, any appearance of favouritism can damage public trust in institutions.

### 9. Further doubts

#### i) An unfair burden
Expecting women leaders to "lift" others places a duty on them that men do not carry.

#### ii) Solidarity assumed
Women differ by class, party and ideology; a woman leader is not obliged to favour women, and voters judge her on her whole record, not on gender alone.

#### iii) Queen bees
Some senior women distance themselves from junior women — the "**queen bee**" pattern described in ==1974==. Later research links this to male-dominated cultures, which shows why **institutional** change matters.

### 10. The Pakistani experience
**Benazir Bhutto** inspired a generation, created women's police stations (==1994==) and appointed women judges, but faced the **double bind** and opposition from those who said a woman could not lead a Muslim state. Women's caucuses show the power of solidarity. In the civil service, a **10%** job quota and growing numbers of women passing the CSS examination are filling the pipeline, but few reach the top. **Justice Ayesha Malik** (==2022==) became the first woman Supreme Court judge, and **Tehmina Janjua** (==2017==) the first woman foreign secretary — each a role model whose visibility encourages younger women to aim higher.

### 11. Drawing the line

#### i) Useful
Mentoring, sponsoring, networking, role modelling and fair rules — **within transparent, merit-based procedures**.

#### ii) Inappropriate
Appointing women **only because** they are women, without regard to ability, or excluding men unfairly.

#### iii) Institutional reform
The surest safeguard against favouritism is fair recruitment and promotion — open advertisements, mixed selection panels and published criteria. Add **quotas** where barriers are entrenched, plus childcare and the enforcement of anti-harassment rules, so that women's progress does not depend on the goodwill of individuals.

### 12. Conclusion
"Pulling each other up" is not preferential treatment; it is **corrective action** that gives women what men's networks have long given men. Done fairly, it reduces tokenism, builds a pipeline and changes institutions. Pakistan's women leaders and caucuses show its value — and the absence of women from the 2025 cabinet shows how much remains to be done.

## Why this answer scores
- Explains **why** the gap exists before judging the suggestion.
- Gives **both sides** and draws a precise **line** between useful and inappropriate help.
- Uses **theory** (Kanter, Eagly and Carli) and **evidence** (India, Pakistan's caucus).
- A **mapping figure** shows how "pulling up" removes specific barriers.
